Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.842 --> 00:00:07.550
SERGEI SOKOLENKO:
Welcome to Cloud OnAir,

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:09.770
live webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:11.370
My name is Sergei Sokolenko.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:13.790
I am the product manager
for Cloud Dataflow.

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:16.280
And today we'll be talking
about advancing serverless

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:18.800
data processing
in Cloud Dataflow,

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.250
introducing distributed shuffle.

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:23.710
You can ask questions
anytime on the platform.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:26.820
We have Googlers standing
by ready to answer them.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.420
Let's get started.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:32.049
I want to spend just a few
minutes talking about Cloud

00:00:32.049 --> 00:00:34.000
Dataflow and what it does.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.920
We are an industry leading
platform for serverless stream

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.020
and batch data processing.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:42.020
Customers use us because
we provide unified batch

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.750
and steaming data
processing capabilities.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:47.439
And we offer them in a
serverless, fully managed way

00:00:47.439 --> 00:00:48.980
so that customers
don't have to worry

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.770
about managing infrastructure.

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:54.920
We have an open-source
program model.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.840
It's called Apache Beam.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:59.090
And customers also
enjoy the ability

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:02.090
to use us when they only
have maybe a few records

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:05.600
per second streams and
ranging up to millions

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.580
of queries per second.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.470
When the team went
ahead and thought

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.780
about how to better
improve Cloud Dataflow

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:17.310
and simplify the data processing
challenges that our customers

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:21.282
faced, we realized we needed
to re-architecture our data

00:01:21.282 --> 00:01:22.740
processing
infrastructure actually,

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:26.640
the way that the industry
is doing data processing.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.670
And to explain why we
needed this re-architecture,

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:33.140
let me tell you a quick story.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:37.150
How did the industry solve
the problem of processing

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:39.920
really large data sets?

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.090
So imagine it's the year 2000
and you have 1 petabyte of data

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:45.130
that you want to process.

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:46.090
Well, who am I kidding?

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:49.090
In the year 2000 a
terabyte size data set

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:50.980
would be considered
really large.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.010
So what do you do if
it's the year 2000

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:56.970
and we have 1 terabyte
of data to process?

00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:01.080
You'd probably use a large
mainframe or a Unix box.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.030
Remember, it's the year 2000.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.480
And you would use SAN,
or Storage Area Network

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.870
storage that was attached
to this big mainframe.

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:14.060
As time progressed and new
technologies became available,

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:16.090
for example,
MapReduce, you would

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:19.060
switch to a cluster
of cheap Linux nodes

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:23.090
where storage was
actually part of the box.

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:25.090
And then as things
moved to the cloud,

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:29.360
you would still use your cheap
Linux infrastructure for a CPU,

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.390
but you would switch to
network attached storage.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:36.510
In 2010, Google
published a Dremel paper

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:40.530
which introduced a novel
concept of processing

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:45.090
large volumes of data by using
distributed storage and compute

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:45.700
clusters.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:49.280
And in 2011, they launched
BigQuery, our flagship data

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:52.260
warehouse that was based
on the Dremel concept.

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:59.690
BigQuery had a replicated
and distributed storage.

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:03.130
It also had a highly
available compute cluster.

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:06.250
But it also had something
that the original Dremel

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:07.810
paper didn't mention.

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:10.450
And that's the distributed
in-memory shuffle

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:15.460
tier that connected both
storage and compute.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:17.550
So the question becomes,
why did BigQuery

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:19.770
introduce this
distributed shuffle tier?

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:24.180
What was it that caused them
to care so much about Shuffle?

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:27.120
Well, let's answer the question
first, what is Shuffle?

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.580
Imagine you have a
very large data set,

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:31.615
and it's a key
value base data set.

00:03:31.615 --> 00:03:34.700
So you have key value pairs.

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:38.750
If the data set is reasonably
sized or small in size,

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:42.260
you can probably group
and join this data set

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:45.230
by sorting it, and
doing it in memory,

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:49.760
either on this box, the single
box that you have, or on disk.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.390
Now, if your data
set is actually

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:54.840
exceeding the capacity of
a single processing node,

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.740
you have to distribute
the processing and sorting

00:03:57.740 --> 00:03:59.720
over multiple processing nodes.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:02.480
And then it becomes tricky.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:04.270
What you want to
basically end up

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:07.690
having is a sorted
set of your data

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:11.920
by key, where keys are assigned
to particular processing nodes,

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:14.620
and all the records
related to these keys

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:18.140
are co-located on the
same physical machine.

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:20.060
And in the process
of sorting, you're

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:24.350
going to be ending in
a Shuffle operation

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:28.370
where records are physically
moved from the boxes they don't

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:32.060
belong to to locations
where they belong to,

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:34.460
basically sorted by key.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:38.460
And this is what a
Shuffle process does.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:41.580
Now, BigQuery processes
large volumes of data,

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.450
and it does it in
a distributed way.

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:46.740
So it made sense for them to
optimize the Shuffle step,

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:49.035
because, as you
can imagine, it's

00:04:49.035 --> 00:04:53.500
such a resource intensive
and time intensive step.

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:55.210
In Cloud Dataflow,
in my product,

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:56.980
we have very similar needs.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:00.250
And our customers have
very similar challenges.

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:02.740
They want to group
and join data,

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:06.310
and we're dealing
with large data sets.

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:11.580
And so we have to deal with
it in a distributed way.

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:13.450
In Dataflow we ask
ourselves the question--

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:16.130
can we apply similar
concepts of a distributed

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:19.560
Shuffle to our own pipelines?

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:23.280
I wanted to walk you through the
life of a batch Cloud Dataflow

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:26.690
pipeline as you submit it
to the Dataflow service.

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:30.360
What happens when you actually
send this job to our service?

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:34.270
On the left hand side I have
the graph of the Cloud Dataflow,

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:36.690
a Cloud Dataflow pipeline.

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:39.420
It's what we call a
directed acyclical graph,

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:42.990
with stages and edges
connecting these stages,

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:45.510
as data flows from
top to bottom.

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:48.990
On the right hand side, I have
a more simplified representation

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:50.170
of this graph.

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:51.870
We have data sources.

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:53.850
We have data transformations.

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:55.530
These are the green boxes.

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:59.280
They can be either filtering,
or projections, segregations.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:02.250
We also have join and
group by operations.

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:06.210
And we have data sinks into
which we write results.

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:09.210
So what happens if you submit
this job to the Cloud Dataflow

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:09.710
service?

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:13.190
Well, first off, we
are going to create

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:16.170
a cluster of processing nodes.

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:19.170
And then we're going to start
reading from data sources.

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:21.840
And as we read the
information or data elements

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:23.990
from data sources, we're
going to start applying

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:25.777
data transformations on them.

00:06:25.777 --> 00:06:28.110
We're going to be doing the
filterings, the projections,

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:30.580
the aggregations.

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:33.080
Once we hit the joins
and the group bys,

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:35.890
we're going to initiate
a Shuffle operation

00:06:35.890 --> 00:06:38.920
and use persistent disk
volumes that are attached

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:41.860
to your processing nodes.

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:44.980
Now, at the end, we are going
to write into our data sinks

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:46.990
and shut down this
processing cluster.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:51.580
In Dataflow, as I
said before, we're

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:53.320
dealing with large
volumes of data.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:55.750
And very similarly
to BigQuery, we

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:57.990
wanted to optimize
the interactions

00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:00.970
between our compute
and storage facilities.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:05.160
So we introduced recently a
separate distributed in-memory

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:08.500
Shuffle component in Dataflow,
and made it completely

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:10.180
transparent to users.

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:13.150
As a user, you continue
writing your pipelines,

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:15.010
and you don't think
about whether you

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:16.720
want to do Shuffle
in a distributed

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:19.020
way or any other way.

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:20.740
You just specify a
parameter that you

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:22.990
want to use a Dataflow
Shuffle component.

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:26.650
And we'll take care from there.

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:31.430
Let me explain the
architecture of our service.

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:34.390
Our user code is still running
on the compute cluster.

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:36.430
And this compute
cluster is connected

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:40.440
through a very large and
fast petabyte network

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:43.840
to the Dataflow
Shuffle component.

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:47.950
The Dataflow Shuffle is deployed
in multiple GCP regions,

00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:50.560
taking care of
regional availability.

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:53.330
In the regions where we
don't have Shuffle deployed,

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:54.850
you can still do
Shuffle operations.

00:07:54.850 --> 00:07:58.420
You are just going to be using
the persistent disk Shuffle

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:00.700
operations available today.

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:02.750
But in the regions
where we are deployed,

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:07.450
we have several components which
take care of the Shuffle job.

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:10.810
First off, we have the auto
zone placement component.

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:13.690
This component is
responsible for picking

00:08:13.690 --> 00:08:17.290
the regional zone in which
to run your Shuffle job.

00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:21.130
It will pick the zone based
on the available capacity

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:25.410
and closeness to your
data sources and sinks.

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:28.730
Now, once the zone is
picked, the Shuffle proxy

00:08:28.730 --> 00:08:32.669
will start interacting
with the code that is

00:08:32.669 --> 00:08:35.780
part of your Dataflow pipeline.

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:39.480
And as part of this
interaction, the Shuffle proxy

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:42.630
will accept the data set
that you want to shuffle,

00:08:42.630 --> 00:08:45.810
and then either pick the
in-memory file system,

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:48.660
if your data set fits
entirely into memory,

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:50.760
or it will partially
distribute data

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:54.450
between the in-memory and
the on-disk file system

00:08:54.450 --> 00:09:00.400
if the data set is too large
to fit into memory entirely.

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:03.610
This is all transparent
to users or developers.

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:05.697
They don't have to
decide how much data

00:09:05.697 --> 00:09:08.280
is going to be shuffled on disk,
versus how much data is going

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:10.380
to be shuffled in memory.

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:14.340
We take care of
all the processing.

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:17.521
One benefit of this approach
is that many customers

00:09:17.521 --> 00:09:19.770
are reporting they are seeing
performance improvements

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:23.080
from this re-architecture.

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:27.240
So if in the past a pipeline
that used persistent disk

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:30.690
shuffle might have
taken you 45, 50 minutes

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:34.020
to process a certain data
set, and if after some

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:37.680
tuning and using SSD
persistent disks,

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:42.390
you might have reduced this time
down to, perhaps, 20 minutes.

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:45.030
With Dataflow Shuffle
the processing time

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:48.940
of such a pipeline is
now closer to 10 minutes.

00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:52.080
The results will not apply
uniformly to every pipeline

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:53.070
in production.

00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:55.920
But these are approximate
performance improvements

00:09:55.920 --> 00:10:00.590
that we are hearing from
a lot of our customers.

00:10:00.590 --> 00:10:03.160
Another benefit of
Dataflow Shuffle

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:07.390
is that we are now able to
scale to much larger data sets.

00:10:07.390 --> 00:10:10.650
In the past with persistent
disk based shuffle

00:10:10.650 --> 00:10:13.080
you were able perhaps
to shuffle maybe

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:16.930
a 50 terabyte large data set.

00:10:16.930 --> 00:10:19.450
And the reason why 50 terabytes
was kind of the magic number

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:22.750
was because after that
volume the interactions

00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:26.080
between your processing nodes,
the physical reshuffling

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:28.030
of data elements,
the coordination

00:10:28.030 --> 00:10:31.630
between the workers
was just too much.

00:10:31.630 --> 00:10:34.720
With the migration to the
Dataflow Shuffle service

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:37.720
you're now able to shuffle
much larger data sets.

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:41.200
Customers are reporting that
they are routinely shuffling

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:42.820
100 terabytes and larger.

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:45.030
And the ceiling gets
larger and larger

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:48.959
with every monthly
release of the service.

00:10:48.959 --> 00:10:50.500
So at this point I
wanted to show you

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:55.570
a demo in which I will
quickly join two data sets.

00:10:55.570 --> 00:10:57.400
They're pretty large data sets.

00:10:57.400 --> 00:11:01.240
In total, they constitute
100 terabytes worth of data.

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:05.860
My pipeline is
relatively simple.

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:07.270
I have two inputs.

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:09.520
They are GCS buckets.

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:12.070
And each of these
buckets, it's actually

00:11:12.070 --> 00:11:14.020
a single bucket
that contains 50,000

00:11:14.020 --> 00:11:18.130
files, 1 gigabyte in size each.

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:20.740
And I'm going to explain what
the contents of these files

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:23.260
are very shortly.

00:11:23.260 --> 00:11:26.290
So these inputs are being
joined in my pipeline.

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:30.260
And I write the outputs,
which is a join result set,

00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:36.100
the result set of a join,
again, into GCS buckets.

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:39.670
On the right hand side you
see the code of my pipeline.

00:11:39.670 --> 00:11:43.240
I used Scala to
develop this pipeline.

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:45.850
And I also relied on a
package developed by one

00:11:45.850 --> 00:11:48.970
of our customers, Spotify.

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:50.470
This package is
called [INAUDIBLE]..

00:11:50.470 --> 00:11:54.160
It makes it really easy
to write pipelines.

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:58.090
I'll quickly explain what
happens in this script.

00:11:58.090 --> 00:12:00.610
If you focus on
the main function,

00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:03.520
I'm defining my left
and right inputs.

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:05.650
That's my input
one and input two.

00:12:05.650 --> 00:12:09.100
I'm then doing a left
outer join of one

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:11.530
of the inputs with
the other input.

00:12:11.530 --> 00:12:14.950
And I am saving the results
of this left outer join

00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:16.780
into a collection of files.

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:25.210
Each of the files in my input
bucket has three colons.

00:12:25.210 --> 00:12:27.210
Remember, each file
is 1 gigabyte large.

00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:32.140
And I have 50,000 files
a gigabyte in size.

00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:34.510
So each of these files
has three columns.

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:36.480
The first column represents
the key, by which

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:38.820
I will be joining my data sets.

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:41.640
The second column is
a sequential number

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:46.260
that just iterates through the
records located in my input

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:47.530
file.

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:50.010
And then the third
column is the payload.

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:52.410
And if I had a
production pipeline,

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:55.140
I would perhaps have a
serialized representation

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:58.590
of a business object here,
perhaps a sales transaction,

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:03.870
or a user clicking on a web
page, anything that makes sense

00:13:03.870 --> 00:13:05.470
to your business.

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:07.530
So with this, let me
switch to demo mode

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:12.270
and show you how easy it is to
initiate a Dataflow job that

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:15.381
runs and uses Dataflow Shuffle
to shuffle a really large data

00:13:15.381 --> 00:13:15.880
set.

00:13:18.445 --> 00:13:21.320
In Shell I've done
some prep work.

00:13:21.320 --> 00:13:24.930
And I've already uploaded all
the files in my input bucket.

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:28.670
I wanted to show you the
command I'll be using

00:13:28.670 --> 00:13:31.370
to initiate the Dataflow job.

00:13:31.370 --> 00:13:35.705
This command is using
the Scala build tool

00:13:35.705 --> 00:13:39.610
to run this particular
class file, the demo.join.

00:13:39.610 --> 00:13:42.140
And I am specifying a
few parameters here.

00:13:42.140 --> 00:13:45.500
First off, I'm going to
tell the project in which I

00:13:45.500 --> 00:13:47.480
want to run this job.

00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:50.480
I also specify to
the Scala build tool

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:53.450
that I want to use my
Dataflow runner, meaning

00:13:53.450 --> 00:13:56.360
I want the job to be executed
in the Dataflow service.

00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:02.340
I'm specifying my inputs.

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:05.010
And the inputs is
actually a Shell variable

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:10.260
which will result
in a filter, kind

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:12.090
of in a pattern
that specifies about

00:14:12.090 --> 00:14:14.760
50 terabytes worth of files.

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:17.970
And lastly, I have
a command that

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:22.350
specifies my Shuffle operation.

00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:26.340
So if you wanted to know
how to launch a Shuffle job,

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:29.970
what you need to know is, just
specify an experiment flag,

00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:32.430
and the flag says Shuffle
mode equals service.

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:35.520
That's all you need to
do to run a Shuffle job.

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:37.620
And incidentally, I also
started the job just

00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:41.580
by playing around
with the parameters.

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:42.450
That's all good.

00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:44.280
In a few seconds you
will see the results

00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:46.590
of this job submission.

00:14:46.590 --> 00:14:48.960
Hopefully it will be a success.

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:52.230
It usually takes between
15 to 30 seconds just

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:56.190
to send the files
back to the service.

00:14:56.190 --> 00:15:00.150
Before the demo I've
done some similar runs

00:15:00.150 --> 00:15:02.770
of 100 terabytes in size.

00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:05.930
So let me show you the
results of the jobs

00:15:05.930 --> 00:15:09.890
that I ran just before the demo.

00:15:09.890 --> 00:15:12.430
Here's the collection
of the Dataflow jobs

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:13.880
that I ran previously.

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:15.450
And let me pick one.

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:17.860
And by the way
already, my job is now

00:15:17.860 --> 00:15:20.270
being run by the service.

00:15:20.270 --> 00:15:22.140
But it's obviously not finished.

00:15:22.140 --> 00:15:25.700
So let me still explore
one of the previous jobs.

00:15:25.700 --> 00:15:27.470
It has been
successfully executed.

00:15:27.470 --> 00:15:29.180
Everything is green here.

00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:31.280
You'll recognize
the pipeline graph.

00:15:31.280 --> 00:15:34.590
It takes two inputs
and produces an output.

00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:39.790
Now, just to show you how many
data elements this pipeline

00:15:39.790 --> 00:15:45.400
crunches through, I
have 50 billion rows,

00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:48.490
which will be joined from
each side of the join.

00:15:48.490 --> 00:15:52.840
So both the right and
the left side of the join

00:15:52.840 --> 00:15:59.090
will have 50 billion rows
of 50 terabytes in size.

00:15:59.090 --> 00:16:02.330
The actual join
operation happens in what

00:16:02.330 --> 00:16:05.640
we call a call group by key.

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:10.900
This particular joint will
create 100 billion groups

00:16:10.900 --> 00:16:13.890
of the same key.

00:16:13.890 --> 00:16:16.890
And then the result
of my join will

00:16:16.890 --> 00:16:19.500
be persisted in a GCS bucket.

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:22.440
So it will actually be a
collection, a large collection,

00:16:22.440 --> 00:16:26.040
probably tens of
thousands of files.

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:30.450
And it will be almost
half a petabyte in size--

00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:36.370
2.5 trillion records and
half a petabyte of data.

00:16:36.370 --> 00:16:41.420
So this entire join took
me only 7 and 1/2 hours

00:16:41.420 --> 00:16:43.780
to run the day before.

00:16:43.780 --> 00:16:46.290
And during this execution
I was able to rely

00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:50.850
on Dataflow's distributed
processing capabilities

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:56.040
by using 1,000 workers to
run my joining, my reading

00:16:56.040 --> 00:17:01.010
from sources, my joining, and
my output into destinations.

00:17:01.010 --> 00:17:05.819
Here's a pipeline graph
of the worker history

00:17:05.819 --> 00:17:08.910
that shows how many workers
we used during the execution

00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:11.819
of this job.

00:17:11.819 --> 00:17:15.425
After a quick spike
to 1,000 workers,

00:17:15.425 --> 00:17:19.436
Dataflow maintained the
number of workers at 1,000.

00:17:19.436 --> 00:17:23.980
The 1,000 here is a
kind of a magic number

00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:25.180
related to my project.

00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:27.839
My quota is limited
to 1,000 workers.

00:17:27.839 --> 00:17:31.120
And so this is why
Dataflow didn't go above.

00:17:31.120 --> 00:17:33.270
So for about seven
to eight hours

00:17:33.270 --> 00:17:37.770
1,000 workers were crunching
through these petabytes of data

00:17:37.770 --> 00:17:43.170
and produced 2.5
trillion output records.

00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:49.660
Similar to this
100 terabyte run,

00:17:49.660 --> 00:17:54.400
I also did several similar runs,
but just different in size.

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:56.710
And I've plotted the
graph that represents

00:17:56.710 --> 00:17:59.710
the duration of these
runs in relation

00:17:59.710 --> 00:18:02.210
to the size of the joins.

00:18:02.210 --> 00:18:04.810
So my 100 terabyte
run is all the way

00:18:04.810 --> 00:18:08.170
up there in the
upper right corner.

00:18:08.170 --> 00:18:10.450
And I also have
two additional runs

00:18:10.450 --> 00:18:14.010
which differ from my 100
terabyte run in size.

00:18:14.010 --> 00:18:18.080
And I always step down like
by 5X reduction in size.

00:18:18.080 --> 00:18:23.800
So the 20 terabyte join
ran for about 100 minutes.

00:18:23.800 --> 00:18:28.940
And the 4 terabyte run ran for
probably less than 20 minutes.

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:33.490
As you can see, Dataflow is
able to scale the execution

00:18:33.490 --> 00:18:37.720
time of my joins
pretty narrowly,

00:18:37.720 --> 00:18:40.060
depending on the
data set size, which

00:18:40.060 --> 00:18:44.050
is a great quality of this
particular Shuffle permutation.

00:18:44.050 --> 00:18:47.920
Usually Shuffle starts
getting slower and slower,

00:18:47.920 --> 00:18:51.940
and becomes non-linear when
you increase the data size.

00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:54.490
The other good quality
of Dataflow Shuffle

00:18:54.490 --> 00:18:59.345
is that the resources used
for the shuffling of this data

00:18:59.345 --> 00:19:01.750
set also scales linearly.

00:19:01.750 --> 00:19:03.650
You don't see it
in the graph here.

00:19:03.650 --> 00:19:06.610
But if you can reproduce the
results in your own tests,

00:19:06.610 --> 00:19:10.740
you would see that the Shuffle
resources also scaled linearly.

00:19:14.220 --> 00:19:16.470
So so far we've talked
about batch pipelines

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:20.310
and how we do shuffles
in batch jobs.

00:19:20.310 --> 00:19:23.610
Dataflow provides the unified
batch and streaming processing

00:19:23.610 --> 00:19:25.000
environment.

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:27.090
And you might ask
yourself, how does this

00:19:27.090 --> 00:19:29.120
apply to the streaming
pipeline as well?

00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:33.650
Do streaming pipelines also
need to be able to do shuffle?

00:19:33.650 --> 00:19:34.790
The answer is yes.

00:19:34.790 --> 00:19:38.300
Just like in batch, streaming
pipelines can also do groupings

00:19:38.300 --> 00:19:39.320
and joins.

00:19:39.320 --> 00:19:40.880
You can group and join streams.

00:19:40.880 --> 00:19:45.980
Or you can group and join
streams and static snapshots.

00:19:45.980 --> 00:19:49.430
And this leads to a need to
have a distributed streaming

00:19:49.430 --> 00:19:50.240
shuffle.

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:52.460
Very similar
arguments apply here,

00:19:52.460 --> 00:19:55.490
as we just discussed
in the batch case.

00:19:55.490 --> 00:19:58.100
In streaming there's also
another consideration

00:19:58.100 --> 00:19:59.470
which is very important.

00:19:59.470 --> 00:20:04.480
And that's the state of
your time window storage.

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:06.940
Now, I'm going to explain
what time windows are,

00:20:06.940 --> 00:20:09.590
and why we need to store data
elements related to these time

00:20:09.590 --> 00:20:10.090
windows.

00:20:10.090 --> 00:20:13.480
But just suffice to say to know
that in streaming customers

00:20:13.480 --> 00:20:16.960
typically want to
aggregate data elements

00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:20.620
based on some time dimension,
some business time dimension.

00:20:20.620 --> 00:20:24.280
And we need to accumulate
the data elements related

00:20:24.280 --> 00:20:27.820
to individual time windows
in this time dimension

00:20:27.820 --> 00:20:31.740
until we can actually
process them.

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:34.550
Let's take a minute to review
the life of a streaming

00:20:34.550 --> 00:20:38.350
pipeline similarly to how we
looked at the life of a batch

00:20:38.350 --> 00:20:39.500
pipeline.

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:42.410
I have a pipeline that
leads from Pub/Sub,

00:20:42.410 --> 00:20:46.470
does a windowing operation
basically, windows my input

00:20:46.470 --> 00:20:50.810
stream by some business
time dimension,

00:20:50.810 --> 00:20:53.620
then does a grouping
of data elements,

00:20:53.620 --> 00:20:57.540
and runs an aggregation
operation account in this case.

00:20:57.540 --> 00:21:00.740
It basically counts the
number of elements in a group

00:21:00.740 --> 00:21:02.690
and stores the
results in BigQuery.

00:21:07.810 --> 00:21:10.030
So let's focus on the group by.

00:21:10.030 --> 00:21:14.020
One thing to remember is,
in a steaming pipeline

00:21:14.020 --> 00:21:17.290
once you submit the job
to the Dataflow service,

00:21:17.290 --> 00:21:22.390
we will divide the streaming job
into three phases, everything

00:21:22.390 --> 00:21:27.190
before the group by,
everything after the group by,

00:21:27.190 --> 00:21:29.290
and then we will have
the group by operation

00:21:29.290 --> 00:21:31.500
which will be implemented
as a streaming shuffle.

00:21:36.120 --> 00:21:39.110
Data flow deals with very
large volumes of data.

00:21:39.110 --> 00:21:41.660
And the way we deal with
these huge volumes of data

00:21:41.660 --> 00:21:45.500
is by parallelizing work
and doing the processing

00:21:45.500 --> 00:21:48.210
on many, many workers.

00:21:48.210 --> 00:21:51.990
The way the parallelize work
is by dividing our input data

00:21:51.990 --> 00:21:52.510
sets.

00:21:52.510 --> 00:21:57.640
Kind of sharding the data inputs
by key, dividing them by key,

00:21:57.640 --> 00:22:02.570
and then assigning key
ranges to individual workers.

00:22:02.570 --> 00:22:07.480
So these key ranges become the
basic unit of scaling for us.

00:22:07.480 --> 00:22:09.820
And we only assign
and distribute

00:22:09.820 --> 00:22:14.800
work between processing nodes
by assigning key ranges.

00:22:14.800 --> 00:22:19.150
And these key ranges become
owned by a particular data

00:22:19.150 --> 00:22:21.040
processing worker.

00:22:21.040 --> 00:22:23.560
You might ask, where
do we take the keys?

00:22:23.560 --> 00:22:27.630
If your streaming pipeline
has a group by key operation,

00:22:27.630 --> 00:22:30.520
we'll take the key that you
specified during the group

00:22:30.520 --> 00:22:31.880
by key.

00:22:31.880 --> 00:22:33.750
If you don't have
a group by key,

00:22:33.750 --> 00:22:38.230
we'll actually auto generate
keys and then partition them

00:22:38.230 --> 00:22:39.212
into key ranges.

00:22:42.310 --> 00:22:44.920
So let's take a look
what happens when

00:22:44.920 --> 00:22:46.450
you start your streaming job.

00:22:46.450 --> 00:22:48.520
First off, we'll create
a processing cluster

00:22:48.520 --> 00:22:52.600
of streaming workers.

00:22:52.600 --> 00:22:55.870
We'll assign the keys to
each individual worker.

00:22:55.870 --> 00:22:59.600
As you see here, the keys of
the before-shuffle operation

00:22:59.600 --> 00:23:02.030
and the keys of the
after-shuffle operation

00:23:02.030 --> 00:23:05.090
are being assigned
to different workers.

00:23:05.090 --> 00:23:09.820
And some workers might
get more work than others.

00:23:09.820 --> 00:23:13.600
Well, they're going to
store the keys and the data

00:23:13.600 --> 00:23:17.200
records associated with these
keys on persistent disks.

00:23:17.200 --> 00:23:20.510
This is how we currently
do streaming shuffle.

00:23:20.510 --> 00:23:22.150
All of the information,
very similar

00:23:22.150 --> 00:23:24.490
to our current implementation
of batch shuffle

00:23:24.490 --> 00:23:27.430
before the Shuffle service.

00:23:27.430 --> 00:23:30.330
We are using persistent
disk volumes in both cases.

00:23:33.150 --> 00:23:35.570
And when we have to scale
our streaming pipeline,

00:23:35.570 --> 00:23:39.020
when, for example, we start
with five processing workers,

00:23:39.020 --> 00:23:42.140
but realize that our
data volume justifies

00:23:42.140 --> 00:23:46.340
the increase of this cluster
to, perhaps, 10 processing

00:23:46.340 --> 00:23:47.930
workers, what we're
going to do is

00:23:47.930 --> 00:23:50.630
we're going to start
shuffling the disks that

00:23:50.630 --> 00:23:54.170
store the key ranges that
contain the data records.

00:23:57.070 --> 00:23:59.640
So the entire data
disk, the entire PD disk

00:23:59.640 --> 00:24:04.240
will be assigned from
one worker to another.

00:24:04.240 --> 00:24:06.180
And our streaming
autoscaling implementation

00:24:06.180 --> 00:24:07.980
works very well for many cases.

00:24:07.980 --> 00:24:09.840
But as you can
imagine, there is a lot

00:24:09.840 --> 00:24:12.510
of shuffling of disks going on.

00:24:12.510 --> 00:24:16.560
And it might not be the
fastest way to reassign work

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:18.990
between processing workers.

00:24:18.990 --> 00:24:22.020
Another thing to remember
is that the number of disks

00:24:22.020 --> 00:24:25.790
used in a current
implementation of Shuffle

00:24:25.790 --> 00:24:29.490
in a streaming pipeline, that
number of disks is fixed.

00:24:29.490 --> 00:24:32.010
And it's determent at the
beginning of the pipeline.

00:24:32.010 --> 00:24:33.840
We typically pick a
very reasonable number

00:24:33.840 --> 00:24:34.740
at the beginning.

00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:36.360
But then we never
change the number

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:39.540
of disks for the duration
of the streaming pipeline.

00:24:39.540 --> 00:24:44.250
The only way for us to give more
disks to a particular worker

00:24:44.250 --> 00:24:47.460
is basically by taking
an existing disk

00:24:47.460 --> 00:24:48.480
from another worker.

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:55.620
So we just covered how we
do group bys and shuffle

00:24:55.620 --> 00:24:56.790
in streaming.

00:24:56.790 --> 00:24:58.680
Let's also look at the
windowing operation,

00:24:58.680 --> 00:25:01.350
because it's quite central
to the concept of a streaming

00:25:01.350 --> 00:25:02.760
pipeline.

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:05.610
To understand what
happens during windowing,

00:25:05.610 --> 00:25:09.120
let's quickly review what
a data stream represents.

00:25:09.120 --> 00:25:14.010
Typically customers have two
important time dimensions

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:15.640
in their data streams.

00:25:15.640 --> 00:25:17.370
The first important
time dimension

00:25:17.370 --> 00:25:19.985
is the business time
of the transaction.

00:25:19.985 --> 00:25:21.140
We call it the event time.

00:25:21.140 --> 00:25:25.740
It typically represents
some business timestamp.

00:25:25.740 --> 00:25:29.720
If you have a sales application,
when was the sale done?

00:25:29.720 --> 00:25:33.090
Was it 8:00 AM, 9:00
AM, some other time?

00:25:33.090 --> 00:25:36.390
If you have a
marketing application,

00:25:36.390 --> 00:25:39.420
when did the customer
interact with your website?

00:25:39.420 --> 00:25:42.060
So it's the click time.

00:25:42.060 --> 00:25:44.700
The other interesting
dimension in data processing

00:25:44.700 --> 00:25:46.570
is the processing time.

00:25:46.570 --> 00:25:51.030
So this is the time when your
data element actually arrives

00:25:51.030 --> 00:25:53.880
at your processing pipeline.

00:25:53.880 --> 00:25:57.000
And interestingly, event
time and processing time

00:25:57.000 --> 00:25:59.130
will always differ for
pretty much every data

00:25:59.130 --> 00:26:00.660
element in your stream.

00:26:00.660 --> 00:26:01.780
And here is why.

00:26:01.780 --> 00:26:05.910
I have an example of three
business transactions

00:26:05.910 --> 00:26:08.970
that all happened at
the same business time.

00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:13.420
So the sale happened at 8:00 AM.

00:26:13.420 --> 00:26:15.870
My green, my blue, and
my yellow data element,

00:26:15.870 --> 00:26:18.930
they all happened
at the same time.

00:26:18.930 --> 00:26:22.020
Now, they actually arrived
at different time points

00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:23.520
in my streaming pipeline.

00:26:23.520 --> 00:26:25.860
You might ask yourself, why?

00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:29.820
For example, if the sale
happened on a mobile device,

00:26:29.820 --> 00:26:32.970
that mobile device might
have had different bandwidth

00:26:32.970 --> 00:26:33.750
capabilities.

00:26:33.750 --> 00:26:35.910
And so as the
record was traveling

00:26:35.910 --> 00:26:38.550
through the networking
infrastructure,

00:26:38.550 --> 00:26:42.070
it took its time and got
delayed by almost an hour.

00:26:42.070 --> 00:26:43.710
Perhaps another
customer, the customer

00:26:43.710 --> 00:26:46.470
who submitted the
yellow transaction,

00:26:46.470 --> 00:26:48.130
was on a plane at that time.

00:26:48.130 --> 00:26:50.130
And so there was
no connectivity.

00:26:50.130 --> 00:26:51.630
And the sale
transaction actually

00:26:51.630 --> 00:26:56.280
didn't get into a network and
into your steaming pipeline

00:26:56.280 --> 00:26:59.920
up until three hours later
after it actually happened.

00:26:59.920 --> 00:27:03.600
So this is why event time
and processing time differs.

00:27:03.600 --> 00:27:06.300
But users actually don't want
to deal with processing time.

00:27:06.300 --> 00:27:08.580
This is not what their
business represents.

00:27:08.580 --> 00:27:12.700
They want to be able to
deal with event time.

00:27:12.700 --> 00:27:15.920
They want to be able to
take the input stream.

00:27:15.920 --> 00:27:21.590
And they would like to be able
to divide it into nice windows

00:27:21.590 --> 00:27:23.570
based on the event
time, and then

00:27:23.570 --> 00:27:27.860
run aggregation operations
based on time windows defined

00:27:27.860 --> 00:27:28.755
on event time.

00:27:32.080 --> 00:27:34.800
So now that we understand
how windowing works,

00:27:34.800 --> 00:27:36.840
let's kind of
conclude and explain

00:27:36.840 --> 00:27:39.360
how we store these data
elements until we can actually

00:27:39.360 --> 00:27:41.352
do anything on them.

00:27:41.352 --> 00:27:43.310
Because there's a difference
between event time

00:27:43.310 --> 00:27:45.770
and processing time, we're
going to have to cache

00:27:45.770 --> 00:27:48.350
and store temporarily
data elements related

00:27:48.350 --> 00:27:49.670
to time windows.

00:27:49.670 --> 00:27:52.760
And we are storing them,
again, on persistent disks,

00:27:52.760 --> 00:27:55.070
just similar to how
we store Shuffle data.

00:27:55.070 --> 00:27:58.790
So the persistent disk volumes
have two functions here.

00:27:58.790 --> 00:28:00.380
They will store
your Shuffle data.

00:28:00.380 --> 00:28:04.610
And they will store temporarily
the time window data,

00:28:04.610 --> 00:28:07.720
up until the time when we see
that no other records related

00:28:07.720 --> 00:28:11.794
to a time window arrive, and
we can trigger our processing

00:28:11.794 --> 00:28:12.710
on these time windows.

00:28:15.830 --> 00:28:19.050
The Dataflow Streaming
Engine is our implementation

00:28:19.050 --> 00:28:21.720
of Dataflow Shuffle, but
in streaming pipelines.

00:28:21.720 --> 00:28:26.100
Very similarly to batch use
cases, we have asked ourselves,

00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:30.000
can we improve the processing
of Shuffle in streaming cases?

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:35.050
And can we improve the window
state storage for streaming?

00:28:35.050 --> 00:28:38.850
The Streaming Engine moves
the processing of state,

00:28:38.850 --> 00:28:43.710
as well as of Shuffle from
the processing workers,

00:28:43.710 --> 00:28:46.620
all the way to our
back end service.

00:28:46.620 --> 00:28:50.670
And in the process,
users get three benefits.

00:28:50.670 --> 00:28:53.450
First off, they gain
smoother autoscaling.

00:28:53.450 --> 00:28:57.660
We don't have to move around
persistent disks anymore.

00:28:57.660 --> 00:29:01.530
The other benefit is, we can
now do much better maintenance

00:29:01.530 --> 00:29:03.240
of our operations.

00:29:03.240 --> 00:29:05.660
We don't have to shut
down user pipelines.

00:29:05.660 --> 00:29:08.100
We can maintain our
back end infrastructure

00:29:08.100 --> 00:29:10.290
without impacting customers.

00:29:10.290 --> 00:29:13.640
And lastly, the user code that
still runs on the processing

00:29:13.640 --> 00:29:16.380
nodes, we are not
moving that one.

00:29:16.380 --> 00:29:19.260
So your user pipeline
still runs on a cluster

00:29:19.260 --> 00:29:21.630
of processing nodes.

00:29:21.630 --> 00:29:24.720
Your code gets more resources
because the operations

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:26.880
that used to run, the
system operations that

00:29:26.880 --> 00:29:29.820
used to run on the
same processing node,

00:29:29.820 --> 00:29:31.770
have been moved to
a back end service.

00:29:31.770 --> 00:29:33.840
And now, suddenly,
your user code

00:29:33.840 --> 00:29:37.540
gets more memory and more CPU.

00:29:37.540 --> 00:29:40.710
Let me give you an example of
how autoscaling was improved

00:29:40.710 --> 00:29:42.140
with the Streaming Engine.

00:29:42.140 --> 00:29:45.180
I have a stream of
data that I collected

00:29:45.180 --> 00:29:47.220
over a period of one hour.

00:29:47.220 --> 00:29:51.840
And that stream ranged from 1
to 10 million bytes per second.

00:29:51.840 --> 00:29:55.760
And I have two distinct spikes,
increases in this data stream,

00:29:55.760 --> 00:29:59.930
somewhere closer to the first
third of the time period,

00:29:59.930 --> 00:30:03.110
and then at the end
of the time period.

00:30:03.110 --> 00:30:05.480
When I launched the job, a
streaming job, a streaming

00:30:05.480 --> 00:30:08.450
pipeline without using
the Streaming Engine,

00:30:08.450 --> 00:30:12.080
I got the following graph
of the number of workers

00:30:12.080 --> 00:30:14.060
that the Dataflow
service decided

00:30:14.060 --> 00:30:17.030
to use to process my data.

00:30:17.030 --> 00:30:19.730
As you would expect,
the number of workers

00:30:19.730 --> 00:30:21.080
initially was quite low.

00:30:21.080 --> 00:30:22.550
I think it was about three.

00:30:22.550 --> 00:30:25.880
But then as the data increased,
and as Dataflow service

00:30:25.880 --> 00:30:27.890
realized that the
backlog was growing

00:30:27.890 --> 00:30:31.460
and we could have
used more resources,

00:30:31.460 --> 00:30:33.350
Dataflow has increased
the number of workers

00:30:33.350 --> 00:30:36.200
all the way to 15, and
kept it at that number

00:30:36.200 --> 00:30:38.720
until the intensity
of my data stream

00:30:38.720 --> 00:30:43.130
declined and we didn't need
that many resources anymore.

00:30:43.130 --> 00:30:46.850
All the way up until
the end of the pipeline,

00:30:46.850 --> 00:30:50.300
or the end of the hour,
when the stream rate,

00:30:50.300 --> 00:30:53.240
again, increased, and we
needed more resources.

00:30:53.240 --> 00:30:59.180
Now, compare this graph with
a graph of a steaming pipeline

00:30:59.180 --> 00:31:00.650
with the Streaming Engine.

00:31:00.650 --> 00:31:03.530
The first thing
you'll see will be

00:31:03.530 --> 00:31:06.590
that the number of streaming
workers that the Dataflow

00:31:06.590 --> 00:31:10.020
service will launch
at the first spike

00:31:10.020 --> 00:31:12.920
is going to be smaller
than in the previous graph.

00:31:12.920 --> 00:31:15.480
And I'm actually going
to overlay the two graphs

00:31:15.480 --> 00:31:17.840
so that you see it
a little bit better.

00:31:17.840 --> 00:31:20.630
So Dataflow didn't
need as many workers

00:31:20.630 --> 00:31:24.410
to process your data, because
now your system operation,

00:31:24.410 --> 00:31:26.780
such as Shuffle
and state storage

00:31:26.780 --> 00:31:29.090
are running on the
back end service.

00:31:29.090 --> 00:31:31.400
Your user code gets
more resources.

00:31:31.400 --> 00:31:34.009
It has a better throughput rate.

00:31:34.009 --> 00:31:36.050
The other thing that you
will see from this graph

00:31:36.050 --> 00:31:39.800
is that we are now able
to much quicker downsize

00:31:39.800 --> 00:31:43.940
the number of workers used
in a streaming pipeline.

00:31:43.940 --> 00:31:52.250
We didn't need to wait all the
way until we saw the reduction

00:31:52.250 --> 00:31:54.720
in the data stream.

00:31:54.720 --> 00:31:59.060
So just to summarize, let me
wrap up and transition to,

00:31:59.060 --> 00:32:01.930
and try to answer a few
questions that some of you

00:32:01.930 --> 00:32:04.850
submitted online.

00:32:04.850 --> 00:32:07.490
The Dataflow team has learned
that using a distributed

00:32:07.490 --> 00:32:10.820
shuffle was really the
key component, a really

00:32:10.820 --> 00:32:14.450
architectural decision for an
effective separation of state

00:32:14.450 --> 00:32:18.620
storage and compute processing.

00:32:18.620 --> 00:32:20.450
We did this in the
batch pipelines.

00:32:20.450 --> 00:32:23.000
We offered this capability
in streaming pipelines.

00:32:23.000 --> 00:32:25.160
In batch we offer it
in Dataflow Shuffle.

00:32:25.160 --> 00:32:26.840
And it allows your
batch pipelines

00:32:26.840 --> 00:32:30.230
to run faster and scale
to larger data sets.

00:32:30.230 --> 00:32:33.390
In the streaming case, we
have the Streaming Engine,

00:32:33.390 --> 00:32:36.440
which also leads to
improvements in autoscaling,

00:32:36.440 --> 00:32:39.330
and also improvements
in separatability.

00:32:39.330 --> 00:32:43.400
These two features are
available in two GCP regions,

00:32:43.400 --> 00:32:46.700
in US-central1 and Europe-west1.

00:32:46.700 --> 00:32:49.640
Dataflow Shuffle is now
in general availability,

00:32:49.640 --> 00:32:51.710
and the Streaming
Engine is in beta.

00:32:51.710 --> 00:32:54.610
If you would like to try one
of these features or Dataflow

00:32:54.610 --> 00:32:59.600
in general, please go to
cloud.google.com/dataflow

00:32:59.600 --> 00:33:03.280
and you'll get all the
information you desire.

00:33:03.280 --> 00:33:05.500
So with this, stay
tuned for the Q&amp;A.

00:33:05.500 --> 00:33:08.740
We'll be back in just a minute
after reviewing the questions

00:33:08.740 --> 00:33:10.287
that were submitted.

00:33:10.287 --> 00:33:12.370
I would be happy to answer
the questions you have.

00:33:20.570 --> 00:33:21.860
Welcome back.

00:33:21.860 --> 00:33:24.030
So we did get quite
a few questions here.

00:33:24.030 --> 00:33:25.890
So let's go through them.

00:33:25.890 --> 00:33:27.590
The first one was,
how do I start

00:33:27.590 --> 00:33:30.230
using the Dataflow Shuffle?

00:33:30.230 --> 00:33:33.950
If you remember from my demo,
I used the parameter that said,

00:33:33.950 --> 00:33:37.520
experiments equals, shuffle
mode equals service.

00:33:37.520 --> 00:33:39.950
That's all you need to do
to start using the Dataflow

00:33:39.950 --> 00:33:41.930
Shuffle feature.

00:33:41.930 --> 00:33:44.210
Incidentally, this is
exactly the same way

00:33:44.210 --> 00:33:48.490
you would specify, with just
slightly different parameter

00:33:48.490 --> 00:33:51.962
name, how would you specify the
use of the Streaming Engine?

00:33:51.962 --> 00:33:53.420
In the Streaming
Engine's case, you

00:33:53.420 --> 00:33:59.400
would say experiments equal
Streaming Engine equals

00:33:59.400 --> 00:34:00.750
a particular value.

00:34:00.750 --> 00:34:04.220
If you would like to know
the exact wording of these

00:34:04.220 --> 00:34:10.070
parameters, just go to the link
I shared in the previous slide,

00:34:10.070 --> 00:34:13.280
cloud.google.com/dataflow.

00:34:13.280 --> 00:34:14.239
Go to our Docs.

00:34:14.239 --> 00:34:16.250
And you'll see the parameters.

00:34:16.250 --> 00:34:17.340
They are right there.

00:34:17.340 --> 00:34:19.130
You don't have to
change your code.

00:34:19.130 --> 00:34:21.260
You only have to
change or specify

00:34:21.260 --> 00:34:26.550
a parameter in the
submission of the pipeline.

00:34:26.550 --> 00:34:28.530
The second question
is about APIs.

00:34:28.530 --> 00:34:30.929
Are there any
special APIs required

00:34:30.929 --> 00:34:33.710
to use Shuffle and
Streaming Engine?

00:34:33.710 --> 00:34:35.370
No special APIs are required.

00:34:35.370 --> 00:34:40.650
You're still thinking in terms
of a direct or cyclical graph.

00:34:40.650 --> 00:34:42.360
You write your
Dataflow pipelines

00:34:42.360 --> 00:34:47.969
using either the Python or Java
SDK using the same commands,

00:34:47.969 --> 00:34:51.840
same transforms that
you are used to.

00:34:51.840 --> 00:34:53.909
Remember, the Shuffle
and Streaming Engine

00:34:53.909 --> 00:34:57.180
will be used
anytime and when you

00:34:57.180 --> 00:34:59.460
decide to use these
two components, anytime

00:34:59.460 --> 00:35:03.270
you use a code group by
key or a group by key.

00:35:03.270 --> 00:35:06.362
These are the two
operations which use

00:35:06.362 --> 00:35:07.570
Shuffle and Streaming Engine.

00:35:10.340 --> 00:35:16.460
The third question is about
the maximum size of data sets

00:35:16.460 --> 00:35:20.000
that a Dataflow Shuffle
operation can support.

00:35:20.000 --> 00:35:23.280
100 terabytes is
not the maximum.

00:35:23.280 --> 00:35:29.310
We've seen customers doing
much more than 100 terabytes.

00:35:29.310 --> 00:35:33.370
So, as I mentioned before,
the ceiling is actually

00:35:33.370 --> 00:35:36.590
being increased every
month, every quarter as we

00:35:36.590 --> 00:35:38.540
do improvements on the service.

00:35:38.540 --> 00:35:41.150
Hopefully by this
time next year I'll

00:35:41.150 --> 00:35:46.360
be able to add a
digit to this number.

00:35:46.360 --> 00:35:48.880
Another question talks
about whether you

00:35:48.880 --> 00:35:52.390
can use the Streaming Engine
without using Dataflow.

00:35:52.390 --> 00:35:55.090
So today, the Streaming
Engine and Dataflow service

00:35:55.090 --> 00:35:56.455
are kind of part of this--

00:35:56.455 --> 00:35:58.510
of a larger offering.

00:35:58.510 --> 00:36:00.820
It's currently not possible
to use the Streaming

00:36:00.820 --> 00:36:03.730
Engine without Dataflow.

00:36:03.730 --> 00:36:06.900
But it's an interesting
consideration.

00:36:06.900 --> 00:36:08.860
Is the Streaming Engine free?

00:36:08.860 --> 00:36:11.090
Do you have to pay for
the Streaming Engine?

00:36:11.090 --> 00:36:15.480
The Streaming Engine does
generate billable units.

00:36:15.480 --> 00:36:18.800
Actually both in the Shuffle
case and the Streaming Engine

00:36:18.800 --> 00:36:23.140
case, the unit of billing
and the unit of usage

00:36:23.140 --> 00:36:26.560
is the amount of bytes
you process and run

00:36:26.560 --> 00:36:29.290
through these services.

00:36:29.290 --> 00:36:32.700
So they scale very
linearly with the data

00:36:32.700 --> 00:36:35.140
sets that you're
trying to process.

00:36:35.140 --> 00:36:39.180
The feature is not free, but
it's very reasonably priced.

00:36:39.180 --> 00:36:41.840
And you can get the pricing
information by going

00:36:41.840 --> 00:36:45.650
to the Dataflow page,
cloud.google.com

00:36:45.650 --> 00:36:46.730
/dataflow/pricing.

00:36:50.380 --> 00:36:53.140
Another question talks
about stateful processing

00:36:53.140 --> 00:36:54.490
with the Streaming Engine.

00:36:54.490 --> 00:36:57.950
Can you do stateful processing
with the Streaming Engine?

00:36:57.950 --> 00:37:00.100
So this is something
that I didn't have time

00:37:00.100 --> 00:37:04.360
to touch upon in the
presentation in the webinar.

00:37:04.360 --> 00:37:07.510
But Dataflow does
have a state API.

00:37:07.510 --> 00:37:10.690
And the state API will actually
work with the Streaming Engine

00:37:10.690 --> 00:37:13.240
where the state
will now be stored

00:37:13.240 --> 00:37:15.970
not on the persistent
disks of your workers,

00:37:15.970 --> 00:37:18.620
but on the back
end of our service.

00:37:18.620 --> 00:37:19.120
So yes.

00:37:19.120 --> 00:37:20.740
You can do stateful processing.

00:37:20.740 --> 00:37:25.190
There's a state API for
streaming pipelines.

00:37:25.190 --> 00:37:29.750
And the last question talks
about joining streaming data.

00:37:29.750 --> 00:37:32.000
Specifically, can you
join streaming data

00:37:32.000 --> 00:37:33.770
with master data?

00:37:33.770 --> 00:37:36.030
So a use case like
that would be, you have

00:37:36.030 --> 00:37:38.330
a stream of sales transactions.

00:37:38.330 --> 00:37:40.400
And you have, like,
a lookup table

00:37:40.400 --> 00:37:45.140
that converts from a
string value to an ID value

00:37:45.140 --> 00:37:48.680
that you will then use
perhaps for storing in a data

00:37:48.680 --> 00:37:50.270
warehouse.

00:37:50.270 --> 00:37:54.800
The answer is it's pretty easy
to accomplish such a join.

00:37:54.800 --> 00:38:00.530
In our terminology I would
recommend doing a side input.

00:38:00.530 --> 00:38:03.420
Side inputs allow you
to define lookup tables,

00:38:03.420 --> 00:38:05.300
so lookup data sets.

00:38:05.300 --> 00:38:08.210
And as long as they
fit into memory

00:38:08.210 --> 00:38:12.260
of a single worker, maybe a
gigabyte or several gigabytes,

00:38:12.260 --> 00:38:15.680
you do have the leverage
of being able to define

00:38:15.680 --> 00:38:17.120
different machine sizes.

00:38:17.120 --> 00:38:20.780
So as long as your data
set is reasonably large,

00:38:20.780 --> 00:38:23.840
not measured in terabytes,
perhaps gigabytes,

00:38:23.840 --> 00:38:28.160
you can do a very, very fast
in-memory join of streaming

00:38:28.160 --> 00:38:29.750
data with master data.

00:38:29.750 --> 00:38:32.780
Now, if your data
set is large, you

00:38:32.780 --> 00:38:35.600
should use the
streaming Shuffle that

00:38:35.600 --> 00:38:40.970
is enabled by you specifying
the code group by key operation

00:38:40.970 --> 00:38:43.530
in your pipeline.

00:38:43.530 --> 00:38:46.986
And with this we
are good for today.

00:38:46.986 --> 00:38:48.860
Thank you very much for
joining this webinar.

00:38:48.860 --> 00:38:53.430
I enjoyed sharing more about
Cloud Dataflow with you.

00:38:53.430 --> 00:38:57.270
Stay tuned for the next webinar,
Unlocking Data Analytics

00:38:57.270 --> 00:39:01.560
and Machine Learning
with BigQuery ML.

00:39:01.560 --> 00:39:04.610
